---
// Virtual office guide 2. Legal points must be checked against gov.uk before publishing (Blueprint 04 · Check before the push).
import Guide from '../../../layouts/Guide.astro';
import { address, formatPrice, virtualOffice, withCover } from '../../../data/facts';
import { guideBySlug } from '../../../data/guides';

const guide = guideBySlug('registered-office-vs-business-address');
const [p20, p40] = virtualOffice.plans;
const dc = virtualOffice.directorsCover;

const title = "Registered Office v Business Address | Maggie O'Neill Centre";
const description =
  "Registered office, business address or director's service address? What each is, which are public, and how one address does all three. Maggie O'Neill Centre.";

const answer =
  "A registered office is your company's official address on the Companies House register. Legal post must be able to reach you there. A business address is any address you trade from or put on your website and invoices. A director's service address is where people can write to you as a director. One address can do all three jobs.";

const faqs = [
  {
    q: 'What is the difference between a registered office and a business address?',
    a: "A registered office is your company's official address. It's shown on the public Companies House register and used for legal post. A business address is the address you trade from or use with customers. It goes on things like your website and invoices. A business address doesn't have to be registered anywhere.",
  },
  {
    q: 'Can my registered office and business address be the same?',
    a: 'Yes. Many small companies use one address for both. It can also be your director’s service address, so one address covers all three.',
  },
  {
    q: 'Does a sole trader need a registered office?',
    a: "No. Only companies and LLPs registered with Companies House need a registered office. A sole trader can still use a business address on their website, invoices and listings. That keeps their home address private.",
  },
  {
    q: 'Is a director’s service address the same as a registered office?',
    a: "No. The registered office belongs to the company. A director's service address belongs to you as a director. Companies House shows it on the public register instead of your home address.",
  },
  {
    q: 'Can I use a virtual office as my registered office?',
    a: "Yes, as long as it's an appropriate address. That means post sent to the company would reach someone acting for it, and they could acknowledge it. A virtual office in a building with staff meets that test. A PO box doesn't.",
  },
  {
    q: "How much is a registered office address at the Maggie O'Neill Centre?",
    a: `A registered office and business address at the Maggie O'Neill Centre costs ${formatPrice(p20.price)} a month. Director's Cover adds a director's service address for ${formatPrice(dc.price)} a month. So one address covers all three for ${formatPrice(withCover(p20))} a month.`,
  },
];
---
<Guide
  guide={guide}
  title={title}
  description={description}
  tagline="Three addresses, one simple answer."
  answer={answer}
  faqs={faqs}
  cta={{
    heading: 'One address for all three, near Liverpool',
    text: `A registered office and business address at ${address.street}, Huyton, from ${formatPrice(p20.price)} a month. Add Director's Cover for ${formatPrice(dc.price)}, and it's your director's service address too.`,
    href: '/services/virtual-office/',
    label: 'Virtual office Liverpool',
  }}
>
  <h2>What is a registered office?</h2>
  <p>
    Every limited company and LLP in the UK has to have a registered office. It's the company's official address on the Companies House
    register. Anyone can see it.
  </p>
  <ul>
    <li>Companies House, HMRC and the courts send official letters there.</li>
    <li>It has to be an appropriate address. Post sent to the company would reach someone acting for it, and they could acknowledge it. A PO box doesn't count.</li>
    <li>It has to be in the same part of the UK the company is registered in. A company registered in England and Wales needs a registered office in England or Wales.</li>
    <li>It goes on your company's website, business letters and order forms, next to your company number.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>What is a business address?</h2>
  <p>
    A business address is the address you use with customers and suppliers. It goes on your website, invoices, letterheads, Google Business Profile
    and online listings. It doesn't have to be registered with anyone.
  </p>
  <p>
    It can be your registered office, your shop or office, or a <a href="/services/virtual-office/">virtual office in Liverpool</a>. Sole
    traders don't have a registered office. So for them, a business address is the main way to keep a home address private.
  </p>

  <h2>What is a director's service address?</h2>
  <p>
    A director's service address is where Companies House and others can write to you as a director. It's shown on the public register. So
    if you used your home, your home is on the register.
  </p>
  <p>
    Companies House also holds your usual residential address. But it isn't shown to the public, as long as you give a separate service address.
    People with significant control (PSCs) give a service address in the same way.
  </p>

  <h2>Registered office, business address and service address compared</h2>
  <div class="table-wrap">
    <table>
      <thead>
        <tr><th scope="col"><span class="visually-hidden">Question</span></th><th scope="col">Registered office</th><th scope="col">Business address</th><th scope="col">Director's service address</th></tr>
      </thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr><th scope="row">Who has one</th><td>Every limited company and LLP</td><td>Any business, including sole traders</td><td>Every company director</td></tr>
        <tr><th scope="row">On the public register</th><td>Yes</td><td>No</td><td>Yes</td></tr>
        <tr><th scope="row">Used for</th><td>Official and legal post</td><td>Customers, suppliers, your website and invoices</td><td>Post for you as a director</td></tr>
        <tr><th scope="row">At Maggie's</th><td>Every plan, from {formatPrice(p20.price)}</td><td>Every plan, from {formatPrice(p20.price)}</td><td>{dc.name}, <span style="white-space:nowrap">+{formatPrice(dc.price)}</span></td></tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>
  </div>

  <h2>Can one address do all three jobs?</h2>
  <p>
    Yes. Most small companies use one address as their registered office, their business address and their directors' service address. It
    keeps things simple. And if that address isn't your home, your home stays off the public register.
  </p>
  <p>
    Every plan at the Maggie O'Neill Centre gives you a registered office and business address. It's at {address.oneLine}. {dc.name} adds the
    director's service address for {formatPrice(dc.price)} a month. So {formatPrice(p20.price)} becomes {formatPrice(withCover(p20))}. The
    {' '}{p40.name} plan at {formatPrice(p40.price)} becomes {formatPrice(withCover(p40))}.
  </p>
  <p class="callout">
    Want to take your home address off Companies House as well? Read our guide on
    {' '}<a href="/blog/home-address-companies-house/">how to keep your home address off Companies House</a>. It explains the two changes to make. Or see
    {' '}<a href="/services/virtual-office/">registered office address plans in Liverpool</a>.
  </p>
</Guide>
